Title:
GhostPack Necromancy: Reforging C# Tools with WasmForge

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post describes extending Praetorian’s WasmForge framework to run heavily signatured C# GhostPack tools (notably Rubeus and Seatbelt) as WebAssembly modules executed by a Go-based host, avoiding the CLR/JIT execution path.  
- The goal is primarily static and some dynamic detection reduction by compiling C# to WASM via .NET NativeAOT-WASI (wasm32-wasip2), then embedding it inside a signed “outer” PE host used for prior Go tooling.  
- It details the engineering required to make real-world C# offensive tooling work under experimental NativeAOT-WASI: a generated C bridge for P/Invoke, a source patcher to rewrite AOT-incompatible patterns and unsupported BCL calls, and a helper library that re-implements missing Windows-centric BCL functionality by proxying to host Win32 calls.  
- A key challenge is wasm32↔x64 ABI mismatch (pointer width/output buffers), solved by routing P/Invokes through DirectPInvoke-linked wrappers and a generic dispatcher (wf_call → host win32_syscalln) with explicit 64-bit argument handling and an out8_mask mechanism.  
- The authors validate substantial command parity for Seatbelt on Windows 11 and common Rubeus verbs in GOAD, while calling out remaining gaps (generic WASI P2 sockets, some BCrypt structured outputs, and general callback/function-pointer thunks).  
- Useful for red teams/offensive tool developers building loaders and compilation pipelines to keep legacy C# tradecraft usable on hardened endpoints; also relevant to defenders tracking emerging WASM-based execution and syscall proxying patterns.  
- It’s notable because it operationalizes “compile-to-WASM to evade .NET-specific telemetry (AMSI/ETW) and signatures” and open-sources the full toolchain.

Technical Focus:
- .NET NativeAOT-WASI (wasm32-wasip2) compilation pipeline
- DirectPInvoke + generated P/Invoke routing/bridging
- wasm32-to-x64 ABI/pointer-width marshaling and output protection (out8_mask)
- BCL replacement via helper shims (Registry/WMI/Pipes/Identity/etc.)
- Host-based Win32 syscall dispatch (win32_syscalln) from WASM guest
- Automated source patching/AST transforms for AOT compatibility

Use Cases:
- Repackaging GhostPack tools (Rubeus/Seatbelt/SharpDPAPI/Certify) to reduce static signatures and CLR-based detections
- Building “signed host / hidden payload” PE wrappers that execute WASM modules on Windows
- Porting other C# tooling to WASI by auto-patching unsupported APIs and substituting helper shims
- Researching/detecting WASM-based loaders that proxy Win32 APIs and bypass AMSI/CLR ETW surfaces
- Creating repeatable, measurement-driven iteration loops for signature reduction/diversification

Keywords:
WasmForge, WebAssembly, WASI Preview 2, wasm32-wasip2, .NET NativeAOT, NativeAOT-LLVM, DirectPInvoke, P/Invoke, GhostPack, Rubeus, Seatbelt, AMSI, ETW, win32_syscalln, Wazero, BCL shims, PlatformNotSupportedException, BCrypt, Kerberos, LSA ticket cache, source patcher